{
  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Detroit Tigers arrived with modest expectations but immediately captured hearts with the emergence of rookie Akil Baddoo, whose walk-off single on Opening Day set the tone for a scrappy, unpredictable club. April was rough — an 8-19 record reflecting a team still finding its legs — but the young talent on the roster gave fans reason to believe something different was brewing in Motown.\n\nMay and June marked a genuine awakening. Miguel Cabrera's grand slam, Robbie Grossman's clutch home runs, and a string of late-game rallies powered the Tigers to a combined 28-26 record over those two months. The bullpen, led by the volatile Gregory Soto, remained a source of anxiety, but the offense — fueled by veterans and youngsters alike — proved capable of answering back. July continued in the same vein, with Jake Rogers' grand slam and Cabrera's timely hitting keeping Detroit above water at 14-12 for the month.\n\nAugust brought the season's most discouraging stretch, a 12-14 slide in which the bullpen repeatedly surrendered late leads — Jo Adell's grand slam off Soto and back-to-back homers allowed by Fulmer were emblematic of a club that couldn't hold the line when it mattered most. Yet the Tigers refused to fold, and September produced a 14-12 bounce-back fueled by Jonathan Schoop's grand slam, Jeimer Candelario's clutch blast, and Bryan Garcia's crucial double play to strand Milwaukee's tying run.\n\nIn the end, Detroit finished 77-85, a meaningful step forward for a rebuilding franchise that had bottomed out just years before. They didn't make the playoffs, but the glimpses of Baddoo, the milestones chased by Cabrera, and the competitive fire shown month after month laid a foundation worth believing in. The 2021 Tigers weren't a postseason team, but they were something almost as valuable: a team that made losing feel temporary.",
